      "text": "To determine the number of students who did not receive an A in either math or history, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\nLet's denote:\n- \\( H \\) as the set of students who received an A in history.\n- \\( M \\) as the set of students who received an A in math.\n\nFrom the problem, we know:\n- \\( |H| = 7 \\) (students who received an A in history).\n- \\( |M| = 13 \\) (students who received an A in math).\n- \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\) (students who received an A in both history and math).\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received at least one A in either history or math. This is given by the formula for the union of two sets:\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThis means that 16 students received at least one A in either history or math.\n\nSince there are 30 students in total, the number of students who did not receive an A in either course is:\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
